      "1 1 1 1 1 1 1 0 0",
      "`###Explanation",
      "**Sample 1, test case 2:**",
      "The simple cost from (2,4)(2, 4)(2,4) to (5,2)(5, 2)(5,2) is ∣2−5∣+∣4−2∣=3+2=5| 2 - 5 | + | 4 - 2 | = 3 + 2 = 5∣2−5∣+∣4−2∣=3+2=5. We can achieve a beauty of 222 without exceeding the simple cost in the following manner:",
      "Perform a move of type 111 from (2,4)(2, 4)(2,4) to (5,4)(5, 4)(5,4) at a cost of 333.",
      "Perform a move of type 111 from (5,4)(5, 4)(5,4) to (5,2)(5, 2)(5,2) at a cost of 222.",
      "**Sample 3, test case 1:**",
      "The simple cost from (1,1)(1, 1)(1,1) to (1,7)(1, 7)(1,7) is ∣1−1∣+∣1−7∣=0+6=6| 1 - 1 | + | 1 - 7 | = 0 + 6 = 6∣1−1∣+∣1−7∣=0+6=6. However we can achieve a beauty of 222 without exceeding the simple cost in the following manner:",
      "Perform a move of type 111 from (1,1)(1, 1)(1,1) to (1,3)(1, 3)(1,3) at a cost of 222.",
      "Perform a move of type 222 from (1,3)(1, 3)(1,3) to (1,5)(1, 5)(1,5) at a cost of 000.",
      "Perform a move of type 111 from (1,5)(1, 5)(1,5) to (1,7)(1, 7)(1,7) at a cost of 222.",
      "Note that the cost of some sequence with beauty &gt;1&gt; 1&gt;1 might be lower than the simple cost.",
      "**Sample 3, test case 2:**",
      "In the second case of third sample, the simple cost from (9,9)(9, 9)(9,9) to (1,9)(1, 9)(1,9) is ∣9−1∣+∣9−9∣=8+0=8| 9 - 1 | + | 9 - 9 | = 8 + 0 = 8∣9−1∣+∣9−9∣=8+0=8. However we can achieve a beauty of 444 without exceeding the simple cost in the following manner:",
      "Perform a move of type 111 from (9,9)(9, 9)(9,9) to (9,7)(9, 7)(9,7) at a cost of 222.",
      "Perform a move of type 222 from (9,7)(9, 7)(9,7) to (1,7)(1, 7)(1,7) at a cost of 000.",
      "Perform a move of type 111 from (1,7)(1, 7)(1,7) to (1,9)(1, 9)(1,9) at a cost of 222.",
      "Perform a move of type 111 from (1,9)(1, 9)(1,9) to (1,7)(1, 7)(1,7) at a cost of 222.",
      "Perform a move of type 111 from (1,7)(1, 7)(1,7) to (1,9)(1, 9)(1,9) at a cost of 222.",
      "Also note that in the same sample the answer for (1,7)(1, 7)(1,7) will be 111, not 333 as the simple cost from (9,9)(9, 9)(9,9) to (1,7)(1, 7)(1,7) is 222. This can be achieved by performing a move of type 111 from (9,9)(9, 9)(9,9) to (9,7)(9, 7)(9,7) at cost of 222 and then a move of type 222 from (9,7)(9, 7)(9,7) to (1,7)(1, 7)(1,7) at a cost of 000."
    ],
    "inputFormat": "The first line contains an integer TTT, the number of test cases. Then TTT test cases follow.\nThe first line of each test case contains an integer NNN.\nThe following NNN lines each contains a string S=s1s2…sNS=s_1 s_2 \\ldots s_NS=s1​s2​…sN​. The string on the iii-th line represents the iii-th row of the grid, where sjs_{j}sj​ is 111 if the cell (i,j)(i, j)(i,j) is land, and 000 if it is water.\nThe last line of each test case contains two integers xax_{a}xa​ and yay_{a}ya​ where (xa,ya)(x_a, y_a)(xa​,ya​) represents the cell where the first contest will be held. It is guaranteed that this cell will be a land cell.",
    "outputFormat": "For each test case, print NNN lines, each containing the NNN space-separated integers. The jjj-th value of the iii-th line represents the maximum beauty possible on a journey from (xa,ya)(x_a, y_a)(xa​,ya​) to (i,j)(i, j)(i,j) satisfying the mentioned conditions, if the cell (i,j)(i, j)(i,j) is land. If (i,j)(i, j)(i,j) is a water cell or belongs to the same island as (xa,ya)(x_a, y_a)(xa​,ya​), the value to be printed is 0.",
